\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Form-Focused Instruction After the Communicative Turn}
\author{M. Alderson}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
The communicative turn in language teaching pushed explicit
grammar instruction to the margins of the classroom, and for
a time methodologists debated whether form-focused teaching
had any place at all \cite{doughty2003}.

\section{The case for explicit instruction}
The empirical record assembled over two decades suggests
that some attention to form is not merely compatible with
communicative teaching but necessary for durable accuracy
\cite{ellis2009}. An influential defence of explicit rule
teaching argues that grammar instruction works best when
learners are led to notice forms in use before the rules are
stated \cite{thornbury2006}.

\section{From rules to resources}
More recent pedagogical grammars treat rules as provisional
generalisations rather than as prescriptions, a position
developed at length in the standard teacher-training text
\cite{larsen2011}.

\section{Conclusion}
The debate is no longer whether to teach grammar but how to
sequence noticing, rule statement and practice within a
communicative syllabus.

